is Ruck"},"license":"MIT","homepage":"https://truss.ruck.io","keywords":["svelte","sveltekit","design-system","ui","components","accessible","material","blueprint"],"repository":{"url":"git+https://github.com/ruckc/truss-ui.git","type":"git"},"description":"Svelte 5 / SvelteKit design system blending Material 3 surfaces with Blueprint density","maintainers":[{"name":"ruckc","email":"curtis.ruck@gmail.com"}],"readme":"# Truss UI\n\n[![CI](https://github.com/ruckc/truss-ui/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/ruckc/truss-ui/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\n[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg)](LICENSE)\n\nA **Svelte 5 / SvelteKit design system** that blends Material 3's tonal surfaces, elevation and rounding with Blueprint's density, 32px controls and hairline structure — built from scratch, with no UI-library dependencies.\n\n**Docs and live demo: <https://truss.ruck.io>**\n\n- **140+ components**: forms and pickers (no native `<select>`, date or time inputs), data tables with virtual scrolling, trees, menus, dialogs, an application shell and more.\n- **Zero runtime dependencies** in the core (peer: `svelte`). Fonts are bundled and served by *your* build.\n- **Accessible by construction**: names, ARIA, focus and complete keyboard maps, audited for every combination of every component's options.\n- **Themeable**: one set of `--tr-*` CSS tokens, light and dark, drives everything, including charts, graphs and maps.\n- **Tested like a library**: over 16,000 real-browser tests sweep option matrices and verify behaviour.\n\n## Packages\n\n| Package | What | Dependencies |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [`@ruckc/truss-ui`](https://truss.ruck.io/docs/getting-started/) | Core: tokens, fonts, icons, components | peer: `svelte` |\n| [`@ruckc/truss-chart`](https://truss.ruck.io/docs/packages/) | Line, bar, donut, histogram, sparkline (SVG) | peer: `svelte` |\n| [`@ruckc/truss-graph`](https://truss.ruck.io/docs/packages/) | Node/edge editor, custom nodes, 5 edge routers, minimap | peers: `svelte`, `@ruckc/truss-ui` |\n| [`@ruckc/truss-ol`](https://truss.ruck.io/docs/packages/) | OpenLayers map styled by the tokens | peers: `svelte`, `@ruckc/truss-ui`, `ol` |\n\n## Quick start\n\n```sh\nnpm install @ruckc/truss-ui\n```\n\n```svelte\n<!-- +layout.svelte -->\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import '@ruckc/truss-ui/styles.css';\n  import { Toaster } from '@ruckc/truss-ui';\n  let { children } = $props();\n</script>\n\n{@render children()}\n<Toaster />\n```\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { Button, Input, DatePicker, toasts } from '@ruckc/truss-ui';\n  let name = $state('');\n  let due = $state<string>();\n</script>\n\n<Input label=\"Name\" bind:value={name} />\n<DatePicker label=\"Due\" bind:value={due} />\n<Button onclick={() => toasts.success(`Saved ${name}`)}>Save</Button>\n```\n\nWith SvelteKit remote functions, controls take a `form()` field's `.as()` spread and its `issues()` (hidden inputs make `Select`, `MultiSelect`, `Combobox`, `NumberInput`, `PhoneInput`, `CurrencyInput`, `CardInput`, `TreeSelect`, `TransferList` and the pickers post natively):\n\n```svelte\n<form {...createTask}>\n  <Input label=\"Title\" {...f.title.as('text')} issues={f.title.issues()} />\n  <Select label=\"Priority\" options={priorities} {...f.priority.as('select')} issues={f.priority.issues()} />\n</form>\n```\n\nSee **[Getting started](https://truss.ruck.io/docs/getting-started/)**, the **[component reference](https://truss.ruck.io/docs/)** (a live playground and generated API table for every component), **[Theming](https://truss.ruck.io/docs/theming/)** and **[Accessibility](https://truss.ruck.io/docs/accessibility/)**.\n\n## Highlights\n\n- **DataTable**: sticky header, resizable and sortable columns, tree rows, checkbox selection, column menu, optional pagination *or* virtual scrolling with infinite loading, per-column filters (a filter row, a heading menu opened by right-click or Shift+F10, or both) and a remote mode for server-side filtering and sorting.\n- **Date and time**: `Calendar`, `DatePicker`, `DateRangePicker`, `TimePicker`, `DateTimePicker`, `DateTimeRangePicker` (one combined popover), `TimeRangePicker`, `DurationInput`.\n- **InputGroup**: joins inputs, selects, pickers, buttons and addons into one seamless control.\n- **Graph**: custom node components, named ports on any side, edge routing (`bezier`, `straight`, `step`, `smoothstep`, `avoid`) and a minimap.\n- **Charts**: horizontal, stacked, 100% stacked and negative bars; histograms with drag-to-brush; every chart has a table view and keyboard readout.\n\n## Development\n\n```sh\nnpm install\nnpm run dev          # demo + docs site at http://localhost:5173\nnpm run check        # svelte-check\nnpm run package:all  # build all four packages into their dist/ folders\nnpm run build        # regenerate the API docs and prerender the site (adapter-static)\nnpm test             # the full browser suite (starts its own dev server)\nnpm run test:coverage  # the suite with V8 coverage; the report lands in coverage/report/index.html\n```\n\nCoverage is collected from the browser (V8, no instrumented build) and mapped back to the `.svelte`/`.ts` sources; `coverage/report/` also holds `lcov.info` and `coverage-summary.json`. To measure a subset, run `COVERAGE=1 npx playwright test tests/misc.spec.ts` and then `npm run coverage:report`. In CI the browser suite is split into 6 parallel shards; a final job merges their test reports and coverage into one HTML report and a summary on the run page (report only, no thresholds).\n\nRepository layout:\n\n```\nsrc/lib/              @ruckc/truss-ui (core)\npackages/charts/      @ruckc/truss-chart\npackages/graph/       @ruckc/truss-graph\npackages/map/         @ruckc/truss-ol\nsrc/routes/           the docs and demo site\nsrc/harness/, tests/  the component matrix and behavioural specs\nscripts/              API-reference and token extraction for the docs\n```\n\nThe API reference is **generated from the source** (`npm run docs`), so it can't drift from the code. The test suite is described in [Testing](https://truss.ruck.io/docs/testing/).\n\n## Releases\n\nEvery push to `main` that passes CI is released automatically: the version is worked out from the commit messages ([Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org)), all four packages are published to npm at that same version, and a GitHub release and `CHANGELOG.md` entry are created.\n\n| Commit | Release |\n| --- | --- |\n| `feat: …` | minor |\n| `fix:`, `perf:`, `revert:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `build:`, `ci:`, `chore:`, `style:` | patch |\n| any type with `!` (e.g. `feat!: …`) or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer | major |\n\nCommits that don't follow the format (no `type:` prefix) don't trigger a release on their own. Publishing uses npm [trusted publishing](https://docs.npmjs.com/trusted-publishers) (OIDC from the `ci.yml` workflow), so no npm token is stored anywhere. Each package's trusted publisher on npmjs.com must be for workflow `ci.yml` with the **`npm publish`** action allowed (the default is only `npm stage publish`, which npm rejects with `403 OIDC permission denied for this action`).\n\nA new version can take several minutes to become installable after the release job publishes it, so an install that returns 404 right after a release should be retried rather than republished.\n\n## Licence\n\nMIT © Curtis Ruck. The bundled Inter and JetBrains Mono fonts are under the SIL Open Font License 1.1.\n","readmeFilename":"README.md"}